---
import Project from "../../layouts/Project.astro";
import DetailSection from "../../components/DetailSection.astro";
---

<Project
  title="Simple Shell in C — Lee Elder"
  description="A UNIX shell in C with fork and exec process control, built-ins, aliases and persistent history. A university group project write-up by Lee Elder."
  ogDescription="A UNIX shell in C with fork and exec process control, built-ins, aliases and persistent history."
  heading="Simple Shell in C"
  note="2025 | Group coursework"
  lede="A UNIX shell with process control, built-ins, aliases and persistent history."
  chips={["C", "POSIX"]}
  icon="terminal"
  tint="peach"
  sourceNote="Source is on the university's private GitLab and cannot be published."
>
  <DetailSection title="Overview">
    <p class="placeholder">
      What the shell does, how many people were in the group, and how long
      you had.
    </p>
  </DetailSection>

  <DetailSection title="My contribution">
    <p class="placeholder">
      Which parts you personally wrote — process control, the built-ins, the
      alias table, history persistence, the parser. Be specific; with no
      public source this section carries the project.
    </p>
  </DetailSection>

  <DetailSection title="How it works">
    <p class="placeholder">
      The read-parse-execute loop, how fork and exec are used to run
      external commands, why built-ins have to run in the parent process,
      and how aliases and history are stored between sessions.
    </p>
  </DetailSection>

  <DetailSection title="Problems I hit">
    <p class="placeholder">
      Zombie processes, signal handling, tokenising input, memory
      management — whichever ones you actually hit. C is unforgiving, so
      there will be a good story here.
    </p>
  </DetailSection>

  <DetailSection title="What I learned">
    <p class="placeholder">
      What working this close to the operating system taught you, and what
      you would build next with it.
    </p>
  </DetailSection>
</Project>
